    id:'p_data_ps_2', subj:'psych_soc', topic:'Data & Reasoning · Psychology & Sociology', title:'Group Size and Helping in a Staged Emergency',
    passage:`Research on the bystander effect suggests that people are less likely to help a person in need when other people are present. Social psychologists conducted a field experiment in a university building to examine how the number of bystanders affects both the likelihood and the speed of helping.

On each trial, a female confederate walking ahead of a lone passerby (the participant) appeared to slip, dropped a box of papers and clutched her ankle while saying, "Ow, I think I twisted it." Other confederates posing as bystanders stood nearby looking at their phones and did not react. The number of confederate bystanders (0, 1, 3 or 6) was assigned at random before each trial, and each participant took part in only one trial. Hidden observers recorded whether the participant offered help within 60 seconds and, for those who helped, the time until they first spoke to or approached the confederate.

Forty trials were completed in each condition. Because participants did not know they were in a study, a researcher approached each one immediately afterward to explain the purpose of the study, answer questions and offer the option of having their data withdrawn. The protocol was approved by the university's institutional review board.`,
    figure:{ caption:'Table 1. Helping behavior by number of confederate bystanders', columns:['Confederate bystanders present','Trials (n)','Helped within 60 s (%)','Mean latency among helpers (s)'], rows:[
      ['0','40','85','12'],
      ['1','40','65','21'],
      ['3','40','45','34'],
      ['6','40','30','48']
    ]},
    questions:[
      { skill:'SIRS 4', q:'By approximately what percentage did the helping rate decrease from the 0-bystander condition to the 6-bystander condition?', choices:['35%','55%','65%','183%'], ans:2, exp:'Relative decrease = (85 − 30)/85 × 100 = 55/85 × 100 ≈ **65%**. The 55% value is the drop in percentage points, not the relative change; 183% divides the change by the smaller value (55/30).' },
      { skill:'SIRS 2', q:'Which concept best explains the decline in helping as the number of bystanders increased?', choices:['Social facilitation','Diffusion of responsibility','Deindividuation','Group polarization'], ans:1, exp:'As more potential helpers are present, each person feels less personally obligated to act, a process called **diffusion of responsibility**. Social facilitation describes improved performance of well-learned tasks in front of others, deindividuation involves loss of self-awareness in crowds (usually linked to antinormative behavior), and group polarization concerns attitudes shifting after group discussion.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 3', q:'Which ethical safeguard is most essential in this study, given that participants were deceived and did not consent beforehand?', choices:['Paying every participant for the time they spent in the trial','Debriefing participants immediately and offering to withdraw their data','Telling participants about the study before the staged accident occurs','Assigning more participants to the condition with no bystanders present'], ans:1, exp:'When deception is used and prior **informed consent** cannot be obtained without ruining the study, prompt **debriefing**, with the option to withdraw one\'s data, is the key protection, as the passage describes. Telling participants beforehand would eliminate the naturalistic behavior being measured, and payment or unequal group sizes do not address the deception.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 4', q:'Which statement best describes how each additional bystander affected the helping rate across the conditions tested?', choices:['Each additional bystander reduced helping by the same amount at every group size','Each additional bystander reduced helping by a smaller amount as group size grew','Each additional bystander reduced helping by a larger amount as group size grew','Additional bystanders reduced helping only when more than three were present'], ans:1, exp:'Helping fell 20 points for the first bystander (85 → 65), 10 points per bystander from 1 to 3 (20/2) and 5 points per bystander from 3 to 6 (15/3), a **diminishing marginal effect**. The total drops look similar between conditions (20, 20, 15), but they span different numbers of added bystanders, so the per-person effect shrinks.' }
    ]
