  {
    id:'p_data_ps_3', subj:'psych_soc', topic:'Data & Reasoning · Psychology & Sociology', title:'Household Income and Self-Rated Health in a National Survey',
    passage:`Across many countries, health follows a social gradient: at each step down the socioeconomic ladder, people tend to report worse health and die earlier. Two broad explanations have been proposed. The social causation hypothesis holds that lower socioeconomic status (SES) produces poorer health through material hardship, stress and health behaviors. The social selection (drift) hypothesis holds that poor health reduces a person's ability to work and therefore lowers income.

A team of sociologists analyzed one year of a cross-sectional national household survey of 6,000 adults. Respondents were grouped into quintiles by household income. Each respondent rated his or her own health as excellent, very good, good, fair or poor, and reported current smoking status and age. Self-rated health is a widely used measure that predicts later mortality even after adjustment for diagnosed disease.

The researchers found a strong negative association between income quintile and the percentage of respondents reporting fair or poor health. They noted that the lowest quintile was also, on average, older than the other quintiles. Response rates to the survey were similar across regions, and sampling weights were applied so that the results represent the national adult population. Differences in self-rated health between men and women and between regions were small compared with the differences across income quintiles.`,
    figure:{ caption:'Table 1. Self-rated health, smoking and age by household income quintile', columns:['Income quintile','Respondents (n)','Fair or poor health (%)','Current smokers (%)','Mean age (years)'], rows:[
      ['1 (lowest)','1,200','28','26','52'],
      ['2','1,180','21','21','49'],
      ['3','1,210','15','17','47'],
      ['4','1,190','11','13','46'],
      ['5 (highest)','1,220','7','9','46']
    ]},
    questions:[
      { skill:'SIRS 4', q:'The proportion of respondents reporting fair or poor health in the lowest quintile is how many times that in the highest quintile?', choices:['0.25','3.0','4.0','21'], ans:2, exp:'28%/7% = **4.0**, a relative comparison of the two proportions. The value 21 is the difference in percentage points (28 − 7), and 0.25 inverts the ratio.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 3', q:'Which limitation of this study design most directly prevents the conclusion that low income causes poor health?', choices:['Self-rated health does not predict any objective health outcome','The cross-sectional design cannot show whether income or health changed first','The sample was too small to detect a difference between income quintiles','The income quintiles contained very different numbers of respondents'], ans:1, exp:'Because income and health were measured at the same time, the data cannot rule out **reverse causation**: poor health could have reduced income (social selection). The passage states that self-rated health predicts mortality, the quintiles were nearly equal in size, and with about 1,200 people per group the sample was ample.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 3', q:'Which analysis would best address the age difference noted by the researchers?', choices:['Excluding the highest income quintile from the analysis','Comparing income quintiles within the same age groups','Reporting the smoking percentages for each age group alone','Increasing the number of respondents in the lowest quintile'], ans:1, exp:'Age is a potential **confounder**, since older people tend to report worse health and were overrepresented in the lowest quintile; stratifying by age (or adjusting for it statistically) compares quintiles among people of similar age. Adding respondents to one quintile or dropping another does nothing to remove the age imbalance.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 2', q:'Which additional finding would most strongly support the social causation hypothesis over the social selection hypothesis?', choices:['Among adults in good health at the start, lower baseline income predicts more decline in health over 10 years','Among adults with chronic illness, income tends to fall in the years after they receive the diagnosis','Among adults of all incomes, smokers report fair or poor health more often than nonsmokers do','Among adults in the highest quintile, those who are older report fair or poor health more often'], ans:0, exp:'Following initially healthy people forward in time establishes that **low income precedes** health decline, which is what social causation predicts and which health-related job loss cannot explain. Falling income after diagnosis is evidence for social selection, and the smoking and age findings do not separate the two hypotheses.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id:'p_data_ps_4', subj:'psych_soc', topic:'Data & Reasoning · Psychology & Sociology', title:'Reinforcement Schedules and Resistance to Extinction',
    passage:`In operant conditioning, the pattern by which responses are reinforced, called the schedule of reinforcement, shapes both how often an animal responds and how long it keeps responding after reinforcement stops (extinction). In continuous reinforcement (CRF), every response is reinforced. In ratio schedules, reinforcement depends on the number of responses, which is either fixed (FR) or varies around an average (VR). In interval schedules, the first response after a period of time is reinforced, and that period is either fixed (FI) or variable (VI).

Fifty food-restricted rats were randomly assigned to five groups of 10 and trained to press a lever for food pellets under one of five schedules: CRF, FR-10, VR-10, FI-60 s or VI-60 s. Training for every rat continued until it had earned a total of 300 pellets. The response rate during the final training session was recorded.

On the following day, each rat was placed in the chamber for a 30-minute extinction session in which lever presses were never reinforced. The total number of presses was counted, and the time at which the rat first went 5 minutes without pressing was recorded as the extinction criterion. Values in Table 1 are group means. All rats were kept at about 85% of their free-feeding body weight throughout the study.`,
    figure:{ caption:'Table 1. Training response rates and extinction performance by reinforcement schedule', columns:['Schedule','Response rate in final training session (presses/min)','Presses during 30-min extinction','Time to extinction criterion (min)'], rows:[
      ['CRF','18','120','8'],
      ['FR-10','42','310','14'],
      ['VR-10','55','980','>30'],
      ['FI-60 s','12','260','18'],
      ['VI-60 s','20','640','27']
    ]},
    questions:[
      { skill:'SIRS 4', q:'During extinction, rats trained on VR-10 made approximately how many times as many presses as rats trained on FR-10?', choices:['0.32','1.3','3.2','670'], ans:2, exp:'980/310 ≈ **3.2**. The value 670 is the difference in presses (980 − 310), 0.32 inverts the ratio, and 1.3 is the ratio of the training response rates (55/42), not of extinction responding.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 2', q:'Which explanation best accounts for the greater resistance to extinction after VR-10 and VI-60 s training than after CRF training?', choices:['Variable schedules deliver more total reinforcers during training than CRF does','Under variable schedules, unreinforced responses are common, so extinction is hard to detect','Variable schedules produce classical conditioning to the lever instead of operant conditioning','Under variable schedules, rats become satiated sooner and therefore respond more slowly'], ans:1, exp:'After variable (partial) reinforcement, long runs of unreinforced responses were normal during training, so the onset of extinction is hard to discriminate from training, producing the **partial reinforcement extinction effect**. Total reinforcers were equal (300) by design, so differences in reinforcer number cannot explain the result.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 3', q:'Why did training continue until every rat had earned 300 pellets, rather than for a fixed number of sessions?', choices:['To make sure every rat responded at the same rate by the end of training','To equate the amount of reinforcement so that the schedule is the main difference between groups','To make sure that the extinction session began at the same time of day for every rat','To prevent the rats on interval schedules from earning more pellets than the ratio groups'], ans:1, exp:'With a fixed number of sessions, groups on richer schedules (such as CRF) would earn far more pellets, **confounding** schedule type with amount of reinforcement; equating reinforcers holds that variable constant. Response rates clearly did differ at the end of training (12 to 55 presses/min), so equal responding was not the goal.' },
      { skill:'SIRS 2', q:'A slot machine pays out after an unpredictable number of plays that averages 20. Based on Table 1, which schedule does it use, and how should players respond when payouts stop?', choices:['Fixed ratio; players should stop quickly after a missed payout','Variable ratio; players should keep playing long after payouts stop','Fixed interval; players should play mostly just before a payout is due','Variable interval; players should play slowly but steadily over time'], ans:1, exp:'Reinforcement after an unpredictable number of responses is a **variable-ratio** schedule, which produced the highest response rate (55 presses/min) and the greatest persistence in extinction (980 presses, criterion not reached in 30 minutes). Variable-interval schedules depend on elapsed time, not the number of plays.' }
    ]
  }
